~/.config/themes/ -- desktop themes
====================================

One folder per theme. Switch with any of:
    ~/.config/hypr/scripts/theme.py set <name>     (or display.py theme <name>)
    the THEME chips in the display panel (SUPER+ALT+D)
    "Theme: <name>" in the launcher's command palette (ALT+E, then ">")
The choice is theme=<name> in ~/.local/state/hypr/display.conf and survives
`hyprctl reload`, gamemode OFF and logins.

    cyberpunk/   the base: today's neon look. Every other theme inherits its
                 theme.conf keys; its stylesheets are empty because the base
                 files (waybar/style.css...) ARE cyberpunk.
    matrix/      phosphor green on black, IBM Plex Mono everywhere, square
                 1px panes, fixed accent #00ff41 (amber warn, red bad);
                 wallpapers: ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-matrix-wallpaper.py
                 --all (digital rain, matrix-1..4.png on all three monitors)
    aero/        Windows 7 Aero Glass: deep-blue frosted glass with a gloss
                 on the top half of every pill and card, 6-8px corners, soft
                 shadows, white text with a faint dark shadow, Selawik (the
                 open Segoe UI stand-in, ~/.local/share/fonts/themes/aero/,
                 README there: source + OFL) with IBM Plex Sans fallback;
                 accent follows the wallpaper but is always tempered into
                 Aero sky blue first (@a-tone, waybar.css header), so a
                 neon pick never shows raw; Nautilus as real frosted glass
                 (gtk4.css + hypr.lua). Wallpapers:
                 ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-aero-wallpaper.py --all
                 (aero-{aurora,blue,harmony,teal,violet}.png, kept cool so
                 the accent never lands on the bad red or warn amber)
    win95/       Windows 95: opaque dimmed grey (#acacac, not #c0c0c0 --
                 HDR), real 3D bevels (inset box-shadows, border width 0),
                 square corners, navy selection under white text, status as
                 solid fills; the bar is a raised taskbar strip with the
                 clock in a sunken tray well and the focused title as the
                 pressed, dithered task button (dither.png); launcher and
                 wofi are the Start menu. W95FA at 12.5px, monochrome
                 (~/.local/share/fonts/themes/win95/, README there: source +
                 OFL; ~/.config/fontconfig/conf.d/60-w95fa-pixels.conf turns
                 its antialiasing off), fixed navy accent, calm animations,
                 Nautilus in Windows 95 colours (gtk4.css). Wallpapers:
                 ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-win95-wallpaper.py --all
                 (win95-teal plus win95-clouds-1..3, drawn from scratch)
    moe/         萌え: pastel sakura. Pink / lavender / peach GLASS bubbles
                 (dimmed for HDR: pills #e8c4d6, cards #dcb6c9) with deep
                 plum text, everything round, Rounded Mplus 1c + Hachi Maru
                 Pop (~/.local/share/fonts/themes/moe/README), lang=ja (the
                 day 10月18日 (日), workspaces 一 二 三, a Japanese launcher
                 prompt), the accent ROLE deep rose (text-safe; the sakura
                 pastel is set explicitly for fills and glows), Papirus with
                 pink folders (icon_theme=Moe), the Catppuccin pink cursor,
                 a sakura-night terminal with a kaomoji prompt, uosc pastel
                 + moe-hud.lua in mpv, sakura / Fuji / yozakura / kawaii
                 wallpapers (make-moe-wallpaper.py --all) and falling petals
                 (make-moe-sakura.py --all)
    lofi/        Lo-fi: a study room at dusk -- a rainy window, a desk
                 lamp, a sleepy cat, vinyl and cassettes. Dark dusky violet
                 glass for HDR (pills luminance 0.018-0.029, cards <= 0.027,
                 Nautilus's view 0.016; only small fills are light) with
                 cream text #e8dccb (never white), soft round corners (pills
                 12, cards 18), slow easing (anim=smooth) and a faint film
                 grain (grain.png, laid over every pane, plus blur_noise in
                 Hyprland's frosting). Nunito for the UI, Comfortaa for
                 headings and the clock, Rec Mono Casual in the terminal
                 (~/.local/share/fonts/themes/lofi/README: sources + OFL;
                 Comfortaa is Fedora's aajohan-comfortaa-fonts). The accent
                 ROLE is a fixed peach lamp #f2a477 (accent_follow=no): a
                 dusk wallpaper's hue vote lands on neon orange (reads as
                 warn) or violet, and violet tempered into peach comes out
                 as the bad berry -- so, like moe, it stays put; the display
                 panel's custom colour still replaces it. Status: sage good,
                 honey warn, berry bad, never the peach; structural magenta
                 is put back to lilac, as aero puts it back to sky. Papirus-
                 Dark with pale orange folders (icon_theme=Lofi), the
                 Catppuccin peach cursor, a Rhodes-and-crackle sound set
                 (make-theme-sounds.py lofi), uosc in peach and lilac +
                 lofi-hud.lua in mpv, a moon-and-music-note prompt, and
                 Miso, a sleepy ginger cat by a rainy window, as the LLM
                 chat's mascot (mascot_lofi.py). Wallpapers drawn from
                 scratch: ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-lofi-wallpaper.py
                 --all (lofi-{rain,desk,rooftop,sunset,vinyl}.png; the top
                 106 px kept dark for the pills' contrast numbers). Panel
                 sizes stay within 2-3% of cyberpunk's (panel.css header):
                 disk 901x1298 vs 900x1279, display 705x1662 vs 717x1663
                 (Theme page), calendar 285x334 vs 286x330, audio 432x388
                 vs 430x378. The ambient soundscape has a "vinyl" kind (a
                 record over the rain) and a "theme" choice that picks it
                 while lofi is on (ambient.py THEME_SOUND)
    luna/        Luna: an original homage to the Windows XP years, the
                 third of the nostalgia set after win95 and aero. The bar
                 is the XP taskbar docked at the top: a mid-blue strip,
                 blue gradient task-button pills, the workspaces as the
                 green round-ended "start" button at the far left, the two
                 centre capsules as tray wells, an orange hot-track glow on
                 hover only, and the focused title / ON toggles as pressed
                 buttons. Panels, the launcher (the Start menu) and
                 Nautilus (Explorer) are cream XP windows with a blue
                 title-bar band; wofi is the dark-blue Alt+Tab box; mako is
                 a balloon tip. Dimmed for HDR: the cards are luminance
                 0.512 (XP's #ece9d8 brought down to #c3bea7, about moe's
                 card), Nautilus's view 0.507, the bar 0.056-0.125, and
                 nothing larger than the toast goes over 0.56. Graphs sit
                 in dark Task Manager wells (#0c140e) and the weather rows
                 in sky wells (#1d4fae), because no colour is >= 3:1 on
                 both the blue bar and the cream cards. Tahoma for the UI
                 -- WINE's open Tahoma (Bitstream Vera based, LGPL-2.1+),
                 not Microsoft's file; ~/.local/share/fonts/themes/luna/
                 README: source, build, licences -- and Liberation Mono in
                 the XP-era PowerShell-blue terminal (Fedora's package). The
                 accent ROLE is a fixed deep selection blue #18439a
                 (accent_follow=no): the green hill would vote green (reads
                 as good) and the autumn one orange (reads as warn); the
                 display panel's custom colour still replaces it. Papirus
                 with yellow, manila folders (icon_theme=Luna, built), the
                 Catppuccin light cursor, bright bell chimes of our own
                 (make-theme-sounds.py luna), uosc with an XP-green
                 timeline, an underline-cursor terminal, and Folio, a
                 cheerful manila folder on a green hill above a tiny
                 taskbar, as the LLM chat's mascot (mascot_luna.py).
                 Wallpapers drawn from scratch -- no Bliss, no Microsoft
                 picture: ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-luna-wallpaper.py
                 --all (luna-{autumn,azure,hill,swoosh,wheat}.png at each
                 screen's logical size; skies <= 0.50 luminance, clouds
                 <= 0.70 and <= 20% of the frame). Panel sizes against
                 cyberpunk are in panel.css's header
    orbit/       Orbit: a mission-control console -- the calm, technical
                 look of a flight control room, over real space
                 photographs. Dark slate-navy consoles (floor #070b14,
                 face #0e1626, wells #16223a), 1 px steel hairlines
                 (#2a3a57, #3d5478 focused), radius 3, no gloss or glow; a
                 2 px teal underline marks focus. Small letter-spaced
                 uppercase labels in Barlow Semi Condensed, values in
                 Barlow (tabular figures where digits change), JetBrains
                 Mono in the terminal and the readouts
                 (~/.local/share/fonts/themes/orbit/README: sources + OFL).
                 The accent ROLE is a fixed "telemetry teal" #5cc8db
                 (accent_follow=no: the nebulae would vote orange, which
                 reads as warn). Status as a control room has it: green GO
                 #4fd18b, amber CAUTION #f2b13c, red WARNING #f0565a only
                 for real problems. The bar is a telemetry strip, the clock
                 a mission clock, the panels console cards. Papirus-Dark
                 with blue-grey (steel) folders (icon_theme=Orbit, built),
                 Quindar-style tones of our own (make-theme-sounds.py
                 orbit: a 2525 Hz beep, the 2525 / 2475 Hz pair, a soft
                 two-tone caution chime; its own 5 kHz low-pass and a lower
                 peak), plain uosc in teal on slate, no mpv HUD, a brown-
                 noise cabin hum as its ambient soundscape (ambient.py
                 THEME_SOUND), and Pip, a little probe with a dish, two
                 solar panels and a blinking status LED, as the LLM chat's
                 mascot (mascot_orbit.py). Generic flight words (CAPCOM,
                 GO / NO-GO, T+, nominal) only: no agency's name, logo,
                 insignia or mission patch anywhere in the UI. Wallpapers:
                 ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-orbit-wallpaper.py --all
                 (orbit-{earthrise,marble,aurora,nightpass,cliffs,pillars,
                 deepfield}.jpg at each screen's logical size; the top
                 106 px faded dark under the bar, highlights capped for
                 HDR), from these photographs (--credits prints this list):
                   earthrise  as08-14-2383   NASA / Bill Anders, Apollo 8
                   marble     as17-148-22727 NASA, Apollo 17 crew
                   aurora     iss072e159172  NASA, ISS Expedition 72 crew
                   nightpass  iss043e059141  NASA, ISS Expedition 43 crew
                     public domain in the US (NASA media guidelines; no
                     endorsement implied); https://images.nasa.gov/details/<id>
                   cliffs     weic2205a      NASA, ESA, CSA, and STScI
                   pillars    weic2216a      NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI;
                                             J. DePasquale, A. Koekemoer,
                                             A. Pagan (STScI)
                   deepfield  weic2209a      NASA, ESA, CSA, and STScI
                     Webb images, CC BY 4.0
                     (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), cropped,
                     scaled and darkened at the top;
                     https://esawebb.org/images/<id>/
    seasons/     Seasons: a field notebook through the year -- calm,
                 rounded, opaque dark plates (radius 10 pills, 14 cards)
                 with a 1 px hairline and small-caps running heads like an
                 almanac's. The first SEASONAL theme: its palette turns with
                 the real date (meteorological: MAM spring in the north,
                 SON in the south) in your hemisphere (display.conf
                 season_hemisphere=auto|north|south; auto = the weather
                 place's latitude, else the time zone's; season_pin= holds
                 one). theme-season.timer runs `theme.py season-check`
                 hourly and re-applies when the season changes; `theme.py
                 season` says which it is and until when (never a place).
                 The accent ROLE is fixed per season (accent_follow=no):
                 spring blossom #f4a8d0, summer lagoon #5ccfd6, autumn
                 copper #ee9a62, winter frost #8ccaf2, each >= 26 dE from
                 that season's good / warn / bad (amber and sun gold would
                 read as warn, so they are never the accent). Pills at
                 luminance 0.007-0.010, wells <= 0.019; text 13.9-15.4:1,
                 the accent 8.3-10.4:1 on the pill (each season's
                 palette.css has its numbers). Alegreya Sans for the UI
                 (true small caps for labels, tnum + lnum for readouts),
                 Source Code Pro in the terminal
                 (~/.local/share/fonts/themes/seasons/README: sources,
                 sha256, OFL). Papirus-Dark with the season's folder colour
                 (icon_theme=Seasons-Spring / -Summer / -Autumn / -Winter,
                 built), a kalimba of our own (make-theme-sounds.py
                 seasons: every note at or below B4), plain uosc in the
                 season's colours, and Sorrel, an original fox kit under a
                 tree whose crown follows the season (blossom, full green,
                 falling leaves, bare snowy branches) with an almanac strip
                 below, as the LLM chat's mascot (mascot_seasons.py).
                 ambient.py keeps the weather rule (no THEME_SOUND: the
                 theme is outdoors). Wallpapers drawn from scratch:
                 ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-seasons-wallpaper.py --all
                 (seasons-<season>-<scene>.png, three per season:
                 spring-{orchard,meadow,shower}, summer-{canopy,coast,
                 field}, autumn-{maples,harvest,mist}, winter-{pines,lake,
                 snowfall}, at each screen's logical size, about 90 s; the
                 top 106 px at luminance <= 0.0051, light accents <= 0.12%
                 of a frame). Each season's theme.conf narrows
                 wallpaper_prefix= to its own three
    icons        icon_theme= in theme.conf (gsettings icon-theme, live in
                 GTK apps; mako's icon-path): cyberpunk Adwaita, aero
                 oxygen (needs `sudo dnf install oxygen-icon-theme`; Adwaita
                 until then), matrix Phosphor, win95 Win95, moe Moe, lofi
                 Lofi, luna Luna, orbit Orbit, seasons Seasons-Spring/
                 -Summer/-Autumn/-Winter (built, folder colour per season).
                 Phosphor, Win95, Moe, Lofi, Luna, Orbit and the four
                 Seasons sets are BUILT, not installed (all but Phosphor
                 and Win95 are Papirus with recoloured folders):
                 ~/.config/hypr/scripts/make-icon-theme.py --all (rerun
                 after installing an app; --sheet <variant> shows a before/
                 after sheet). Win95 inherits Chicago95
                 (~/.local/share/icons/Chicago95/README: source, licence,
                 reinstall) and adds the apps it lacks, aliased to a
                 Chicago95 picture or put through the 16-colour filter
    tools/statebar.py      a test bar with every pill state, for screenshots
                           (`statebar.py shot FILE.png`; deterministic, so two
                           shots of one theme compare pixel for pixel)

A theme folder
--------------
    theme.conf    key=value; every key optional; cyberpunk/theme.conf lists
                  all of them with today's values and comments. font= and
                  font_mono= may be a list, first choice first
                  ("font=Selawik, IBM Plex Sans")
    waybar.css    bar + toolbox   (overrides waybar/style.css, style-toolbox.css)
    panel.css     popup panels    (overrides waybar/panel.css)
    launcher.css  launcher, quick note, clipboard (overrides hypr/scripts/launcher.css)
    llm-chat.css  the LLM chat window (overrides hypr/scripts/
                  llm-chat.css; an open window restyles itself on a
                  switch). Its mascot is per theme too, in code:
                  hypr/scripts/mascots.py (moe_mascot.py, mascot_<theme>.py;
                  `mascots.py --check` after changing one)
    wofi.css      wofi            (overrides wofi/style.css)
    mako          mako fragment   (key=value lines, may repeat [sections])
    gtk4.css      libadwaita / GTK4 (becomes ~/.config/gtk-4.0/gtk.css;
                  :root { --window-bg-color: ...; --accent-bg-color: ...; })
                  read when a GTK4 app STARTS: Nautilus is one process for
                  all its windows. `theme.py set` restarts it when it has
                  no window open, else waits for the last one to close
                  (the toast offers "Restart Files now"); gsettings parts
                  change live
    mpv.conf      mpv's colours, faces and shape (uosc-color/opacity,
                  radius, osd-/sub- font and colour). How the player's
                  interface BEHAVES is shared: mpv-base.conf in this folder
                  is written above every theme's mpv.conf (when controls
                  show, the controls row, message and subtitle sizes;
                  nothing over playing video). A theme line still wins.
    hypr.lua      optional: return { window_rules = {...}, layer_rules = {...} }
                  enabled only while this theme is current (lua/display.lua)
    season/       optional: a SEASONAL theme. season/<spring|summer|autumn|winter>/
                  each hold theme.conf (only colours and the sets that carry
                  them: accent, gtk_accent_color, icon_theme,
                  wallpaper_prefix, wallpaper_video, glass, term.* colours,
                  cosmic.* colours, color.*), palette.css (@define-color
                  lines theme.py puts BEFORE the theme's own css in every
                  GTK3 stylesheet), and optional additions to any file above
                  (appended after the theme's; mako merged section-wise).
                  Not hypr.lua or sounds/, which are the theme's all year.
                  The season follows the date (meteorological: MAM spring
                  in the north, SON in the south) and the hemisphere
                  (display.conf season_hemisphere=auto|north|south; auto =
                  the weather place's latitude, else the time zone's);
                  season_pin= holds one. theme-season.timer runs
                  `theme.py season-check` hourly and re-applies when the
                  season changes. `theme.py season` shows it; `check`
                  checks all four.
Missing file = no override. After editing any of them: theme.py apply.
Before switching to a new theme: theme.py check <name>.

How the overrides work (the short version; theme.py's docstring and the
header of each base stylesheet have the long one): every base stylesheet
starts with a PALETTE of @define-color names and uses only those names in
its rules. The theme's css is imported LAST, after the wallpaper accent, and
GTK3 resolves a named colour by its LAST definition -- so redefining
`@define-color surface ...;` recolours every pill. Anything that is not a
colour (radius, border width, font size, padding) is changed by repeating the
base file's selector, which wins by coming later. font= / font_mono= in
theme.conf swap the face in every base rule automatically.

Rules every theme keeps
-----------------------
  * @cyan is the ACCENT role whatever its hue; set the theme's base accent
    with accent= in theme.conf rather than in css, so the wallpaper accent
    can still replace it when accent_follow=yes.
  * Status colours stay distinguishable: good (green, @good / @good-fg),
    warn (yellow, @warn / @warn-fg), bad (magenta-red, @bad / @bad-fg /
    @critical). DP-1 is HDR: large light areas must be dimmed (no
    full-white surfaces).
  * The bar stays at the top; nothing here moves it.
  * Panel sizes follow the theme's font. A narrower face (Selawik in aero,
    W95FA in win95) makes most panels a little smaller than in cyberpunk --
    aero measured disk 832x589 vs 861x628, display 432x507 vs 432x562 (its
    THEME chips fit one row), calendar 277x310 vs 286x330, audio 400x360
    vs 400x376; the CARD_HEIGHT=412 cards (CPU, GPU, network, updates,
    notifications) do not change. So check a panel layout change in more
    than one theme, and never take one theme's sizes as the reference.
